<section image-layout="blank" id="quYW4NdtYd" background-color="palette-4"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="4HU-H1xZpT" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow">Visual communication history</p><h1 font-size="40pt">The Module, the Rule, the Model: Visual Transformation Across Three Media Regimes</h1><p font-size="lg">How design moved from manufactured objects, to executable systems, to statistical models.</p><spacer size="24pt"></spacer><hr color="accent" thickness="md" width="xs"><p font-size="sm">University lecture · Print civilization → digital era → computational intelligence</p></grid-item><grid-item id="NLT5QD4dH0" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/UrRQolSJChRI19u0XY53u.png" saved-media-id="kym9x3pykkgygyl" source="image.ai-generated" fill="true" image-style="editorial-media-study" alt="Large-format editorial studio photograph of one media mechanism: a fixed paper frame, a modular screen window and a field of neutral components progressively separating from a designer's hand, arranged as a precise material study under cool raking daylight, carbon black, archive buff, screen grey and one vermilion intervention on warm paper, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="CoANLxkicU"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="KnC0fuZZgu"><h2>Every media revolution moves the designer one step further from the artifact</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="IOGUyVTVHd" background-color="palette-2" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Print</p><display size="md">Module</display><p>European metal-type printing industrialized the reusable letter. A decision became a manufactured part that could be cast once and repeated at scale.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="tFEFJ1-OoW" background-color="palette-3" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Digital</p><display size="md">Rule</display><p>Grids, page-description code, stylesheets and variable fonts turned layout into instructions that generate pages the designer may never draw.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="KeKRIj-HId" background-color="accent" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Computational</p><display size="md">Model</display><p>Generative systems accept intent, return candidates, and make prompting, curating and rejecting part of the deliverable.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>The claim to test:</b> each shift traded direct control for reach, and each time the discipline argued about what was lost.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="xN7xC1FDU-"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="kLHT2J-5um"><h2>Five dates that reorganized the discipline</h2><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><smart-layout variant="minimalTimeline" orientation="horizontal" two-sided="true" cell-size="15"><smart-layout-item><h4>1455</h4><p>Mainz scales metal type in Europe. Printing itself is much older.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>1928</h4><p>Tschichold makes the page a system in <i>Die neue Typographie</i>.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>1964</h4><p>Gerstner makes the design a programme.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>1985</h4><p>PageMaker collapses the print trades into one desk.</p></smart-layout-item><smart-layout-item><h4>2016</h4><p>Variable fonts turn type into a continuous space.</p></smart-layout-item></smart-layout><spacer mode="fill"></spacer><p role="caption">Teaching frame for European and industrial practice, not a chronology of where printing originated. Sources: <a href="https://guides.loc.gov/gutenberg/introduction">Library of Congress</a> · <a href="https://idp.bl.uk/discover/learning/dunhuang/collection-items/cave-17-the-library-cave/">British Library</a> · <a href="https://www.lars-mueller-publishers.com/designing-programmes">Lars Müller Publishers</a> · <a href="https://www.historyofinformation.com/detail.php?entryid=1235">History of Information</a> · <a href="https://opensource.googleblog.com/2016/09/introducing-opentype-font-variations.html">Google Open Source Blog</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="NMCZOMwFDm" vertical-align="center" background-color="accent"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="-n44ae0erL" vertical-align="center" colspan="18"><display size="lg">I</display><p role="eyebrow">Media regime one</p></grid-item><grid-item id="W_0-vrKytH" vertical-align="center" colspan="42"><h1>European metal type: the letter as a manufactured part</h1><p font-size="lg">The module precedes Europe. Mainz matters because the workshop scales it into an industry.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="zro-m7LMwE"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="cOEnI8P7uv"><h2>Mainz industrialized the module; it did not invent it</h2><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="fill" padding="12pt 18pt"><grid-item id="X1ZK2pIzAw" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="solid">11 May 868</label></labels><h4>Woodblock printing in China</h4><p>The Chinese <i>Diamond Sutra</i> from Dunhuang is the earliest dated printed book; its colophon gives the date.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="l4WEnAd_zA" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="solid">1041–48 · 13th century</label></labels><h4>Reusable type in East Asia</h4><p>Bi Sheng cut individual baked-clay characters. Cast-metal type was in use in Korea from the 13th century.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="wV-QmFr1qX" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="solid">July 1377</label></labels><h4>The <i>Jikji</i></h4><p>Printed at Heungdeoksa, it is the oldest surviving movable metal-type book, 78 years before the 42-line Bible.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="chU5-w2JWz" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><labels><label variant="solid">Mainz · 1455</label></labels><h4>Industrial adoption in Europe</h4><p>Gutenberg combined cast metal sorts, oily ink and the press into a workshop capable of a large edition.</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>Interpretation:</b> what spread from Mainz was industrial adoption. The visual consequences follow from that scale.</p><p role="caption"><span text-color="muted">Sources: <a href="https://idp.bl.uk/discover/learning/dunhuang/collection-items/cave-17-the-library-cave/">British Library</a> · <a href="https://www.historyofinformation.com/detail.php?id=19">History of Information</a> · <a href="https://afe.easia.columbia.edu/songdynasty-module/tech-printing.html">Columbia University</a> · <a href="https://www.unesco.org/en/memory-world/baegun-hwasang-chorok-buljo-jikji-simche-yojeol-volii-second-volume-anthology-great-buddhist-priests?hub=1081">UNESCO</a> · <a href="https://www.bnf.fr/en/jikji-treasure-world-printing">BnF</a> · <a href="https://guides.loc.gov/gutenberg/introduction">Library of Congress</a></span></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="7IYYVJWgO2"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="_m-mDcBJuY"><h2>The 42-line Bible was a factory imitating a scriptorium</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border"><grid-item id="Doi6ti202M" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md">16,000</display><h4>Pieces of type</h4><p>The modular inventory behind the edition.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="lpR7C-eFvq" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md">50,000</display><h4>Sheets of paper</h4><p>Nearly this many passed through the workshop.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="IlFhsz4Faw" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><display size="md">≈180</display><h4>Copies</h4><p>Around 180, with some 45 on parchment.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="gJ0ueKzWy1" cell-style="subtle" colspan="30"><h3>Production changed mid-run</h3><p>About three years of work. Scholars read pages set twice as evidence that the run enlarged during production, an increase estimated near 30%.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="5Os8k92F46" background-color="palette-2" colspan="30"><h3>The surface stayed familiar</h3><p>Dense black textura and hand-finished initials imitated German manuscript hands. A new medium's first masterpiece often resembles the thing it displaces.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption">Sources: <a href="https://specialcollections-blog.lib.cam.ac.uk/?p=13354">Cambridge University Library</a> · <a href="https://guides.loc.gov/gutenberg/introduction">Library of Congress</a> · <a href="https://webs.uab.cat/aurelialabert/wp-content/uploads/sites/108/2024/08/34.Rangel-Alabert2022JPHS3.pdf">Rangel and Alabert</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="Es14p0m5NT"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="pmp3O6qar0"><h2>The page became an interface</h2><p>Near-identical copies made reference possible. Across the century after 1455, European printed books accumulated a standard kit for moving through fixed text.</p><grid spacing="small" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="ZTWGTQhUGL" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Title page</h4><p>Names the object and its authority.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="_XYaq81eJb" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Page numbers</h4><p>Give every copy the same address.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="kvTQWr-KQq" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Running heads</h4><p>Keep location visible while reading.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="olErFOJ5YI" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Contents</h4><p>Expose the structure before entry.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="FwvLIlFsHJ" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Indexes</h4><p>Turn terms into routes through the book.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="iLCEvvEEw3" cell-style="subtle" colspan="20"><h4>Footnotes</h4><p>Attach claims to other fixed texts.</p></grid-item></grid><p>Elizabeth Eisenstein called this <b>typographic fixity</b>. Her argument, rather than a measurement, is that text which stays put can be cited, corrected and disputed across cities and generations.</p><p><b>Design consequence:</b> standardization is the precondition for comparison.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="eWo12wUepL"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="1_FpoivTrq"><h2>The grid: a moral argument disguised as a layout tool</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="POGMYxfZVk" background-color="palette-2" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">The claim</p><h3>Rules could make design objective</h3><p>Tschichold's 1928 programme made asymmetry, sans serif and the flush-left rag an ethical position against ornament and nationalism.</p><p>Müller-Brockmann's <i>Grid Systems</i>, 1981, cast the designer as a neutral servant introducing order.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="inQvpGhXSz" cell-style="outline" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">The critique</p><h3>Neutrality has a history</h3><p>Identical rules applied to any content do not escape taste. The Swiss grid is a mid-century European aesthetic, not a law of perception.</p><p>Its clarity is real, but so is the cultural position it presents as universal.</p></grid-item></grid><aside><p><b>The structural change matters most:</b> the designer authors a system and delegates the individual page to it.</p></aside></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="flZU5G7HbF"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="-GSIGJLMc4" align="center"><h2 align="left">Gerstner, 1964: designing programmes, before the tools existed</h2><p align="left">Across four essays, Karl Gerstner proposed a decisive move: stop designing the solution; design the <b>set of all acceptable solutions</b>, then select from it.</p><diagram template-key="ideas" align="center" width="780pt" height="230pt" data-meta="{&quot;general&quot;:[{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Set of all acceptable solutions</h4>&quot;}],&quot;step&quot;:[{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Parameters define the range</h4>&quot;,&quot;icon&quot;:{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/sliders.svg&quot;,&quot;tempUrl&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;meta&quot;:{&quot;height&quot;:512,&quot;width&quot;:512},&quot;query&quot;:&quot;sliders&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;image.icon&quot;,&quot;loadImageStatus&quot;:&quot;done&quot;,&quot;uploadStatus&quot;:3}},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Identity rules coordinate variation</h4>&quot;,&quot;icon&quot;:{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/grid.svg&quot;,&quot;tempUrl&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;meta&quot;:{&quot;height&quot;:512,&quot;width&quot;:448},&quot;query&quot;:&quot;grid&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;image.icon&quot;,&quot;loadImageStatus&quot;:&quot;done&quot;,&quot;uploadStatus&quot;:3}},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Modular typeface describes forms</h4>&quot;,&quot;icon&quot;:{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/font.svg&quot;,&quot;tempUrl&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;meta&quot;:{&quot;height&quot;:512,&quot;width&quot;:448},&quot;query&quot;:&quot;type&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;image.icon&quot;,&quot;loadImageStatus&quot;:&quot;done&quot;,&quot;uploadStatus&quot;:3}},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Selection chooses one result</h4>&quot;,&quot;icon&quot;:{&quot;src&quot;:&quot;https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/_app_static/icons/v0/check.svg&quot;,&quot;tempUrl&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;meta&quot;:{&quot;height&quot;:512,&quot;width&quot;:448},&quot;query&quot;:&quot;checkmark&quot;,&quot;source&quot;:&quot;image.icon&quot;,&quot;loadImageStatus&quot;:&quot;done&quot;,&quot;uploadStatus&quot;:3}}]}"></diagram><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="gDiFt-DfKF" colspan="30"><p>A parameter-driven identity or modular typeface describes a space of layouts—not a single layout.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="_-CSVyW7Ef" colspan="30"><p><b>The hinge:</b> programmatic design was the idea; computers industrialized it, but did not create it.</p></grid-item></grid><p role="caption" align="left"><span text-color="#5D6466">Source: </span><a href="https://www.lars-mueller-publishers.com/designing-programmes">Lars Müller Publishers, <i>Designing Programmes</i></a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="iR-blQ4lNh" vertical-align="center" background-color="palette-3"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="htHxudWnrD" vertical-align="center" colspan="18"><display size="lg">II</display><p role="eyebrow">Media regime two</p></grid-item><grid-item id="4uiSw6q9lk" vertical-align="center" colspan="42"><h1>The digital era: the rule becomes executable</h1><p font-size="lg">The system stops being an instruction to a trade and becomes something the machine can run.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="-jkK8q304N" vertical-align="center"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="ROyAUM3R5o" vertical-align="center" colspan="23"><h2>1985: three products collapse five centuries of trades</h2><p>Paul Brainerd called desktop publishing a <b>three-legged stool</b>: the Macintosh of 1984, Adobe PostScript, and Aldus PageMaker, released July 1985.</p><p>PostScript mattered most. A page became resolution-independent code, printable anywhere.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="RnHlmMlpgj" align="center" vertical-align="center" colspan="37"><diagram template-key="inputs" align="center" width="520pt" height="330pt" data-meta="{&quot;general&quot;:[{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Desktop publishing</h4>&quot;}],&quot;step&quot;:[{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Macintosh</h4><p>Personal visual workstation</p>&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Adobe PostScript</h4><p>Page described as code</p>&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Aldus PageMaker</h4><p>Page assembly (July 1985)</p>&quot;},{&quot;text&quot;:&quot;<h4>Displaced roles</h4><p>Compositor, typesetter, paste-up artist, platemaker</p>&quot;}]}"></diagram><p role="caption">Enormous access, real deskilling. Aldus took its name from Venetian printer Aldus Manutius, reaching for old legitimacy.</p></grid-item></grid><notes><p>Sources: Paul Brainerd oral history, Computer History Museum; History of Information; Aldus Corporation history.</p></notes></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="C8lWA4myOm"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="ftTFuwr_uD"><h2>Emigre: designing with the machine's grain</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="dpgrJoRo4K" vertical-align="center" colspan="22"><p>Early screens and printers offered coarse pixel grids. Much of the profession treated that grain as a degraded version of typography.</p><blockquote><p font-size="lg">you read best what you read most</p></blockquote><p role="caption">Zuzana Licko</p><p>Licko's bitmap faces took the pixel as the module. <i>Emigre</i> magazine turned that choice into a public quarrel about legibility, authorship and taste.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="R0iR0cUTqQ" vertical-align="center" colspan="38"><custom-code>
            <style>
              .grain-study { height: 18em; font-family: var(--body-font); color: var(--body-color); }
              .grain-study svg { width: 100%; height: 100%; display: block; }
              .grain-study .frame { fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--card-color) 82%, var(--accent-color)); stroke: var(--body-color); stroke-width: 1.5; }
              .grain-study .gridline { stroke: color-mix(in srgb, var(--body-color) 22%, transparent); stroke-width: 1; }
              .grain-study .pixel { fill: var(--heading-color); }
              .grain-study .hot { fill: var(--accent-color); }
              .grain-study .label { fill: var(--body-color); font-family: var(--body-font); font-size: 13px; letter-spacing: 1px; }
              .grain-study .arrow { stroke: var(--accent-color); stroke-width: 4; fill: none; }
            </style>
            <div class="grain-study">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 620 290" width="620" height="290" role="img" aria-label="Conceptual study of the pixel grid becoming a design language">
                <rect class="frame" x="18" y="30" width="244" height="220" rx="3"></rect>
                <text class="label" x="18" y="18">CONSTRAINT</text>
                <g>
                  <path class="gridline" d="M42 30V250 M66 30V250 M90 30V250 M114 30V250 M138 30V250 M162 30V250 M186 30V250 M210 30V250 M234 30V250"></path>
                  <path class="gridline" d="M18 54H262 M18 78H262 M18 102H262 M18 126H262 M18 150H262 M18 174H262 M18 198H262 M18 222H262"></path>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="42" y="54" width="72" height="24"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="42" y="78" width="24" height="120"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="66" y="102" width="96" height="24"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="66" y="174" width="120" height="24"></rect>
                  <rect class="hot" x="186" y="174" width="24" height="24"></rect>
                </g>
                <path class="arrow" d="M286 140H332"></path>
                <path class="arrow" d="M322 130L334 140L322 150"></path>
                <rect class="frame" x="356" y="30" width="244" height="220" rx="3"></rect>
                <text class="label" x="356" y="18">LANGUAGE</text>
                <g>
                  <rect class="hot" x="380" y="54" width="28" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="414" y="54" width="62" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="482" y="54" width="94" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="380" y="94" width="96" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="hot" x="482" y="94" width="28" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="516" y="94" width="60" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="380" y="134" width="42" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="428" y="134" width="100" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="hot" x="534" y="134" width="42" height="28"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="380" y="174" width="72" height="52"></rect>
                  <rect class="hot" x="458" y="174" width="28" height="52"></rect>
                  <rect class="pixel" x="492" y="174" width="84" height="52"></rect>
                </g>
              </svg>
            </div>
          </custom-code><p role="caption">Conceptual pixel study. Transferable method: when a medium looks ugly, ask whether you are seeing a defect or an unfamiliar grain.</p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="kGLjHALKQ7"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="hRkRlpoRm7"><h2>2010: the page dissolves, and layout becomes a rule you cannot preview</h2><p>Ethan Marcotte published “Responsive Web Design” on 25 May 2010, after naming the idea in a talk that April. Three ingredients changed the unit of approval: <b>fluid grids, flexible images, media queries</b>.</p><custom-code>
<style>
.responsive-study { height: 13.5em; font-family: var(--body-font); color: var(--body-color); display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.5fr 1fr 0.62fr; gap: 1.1em; align-items: end; }
.responsive-study .device { border: 0.1em solid var(--heading-color); padding: 0.55em; background: var(--card-color); }
.responsive-study .desktop { height: 11em; }
.responsive-study .tablet { height: 9.5em; }
.responsive-study .phone { height: 8em; }
.responsive-study .bar { height: 0.55em; background: var(--accent-color); margin-bottom: 0.55em; }
.responsive-study .canvas { height: calc(100% - 1.1em); display: grid; gap: 0.45em; }
.responsive-study .desktop .canvas { grid-template-columns: 1.5fr 1fr; grid-template-rows: 1fr 0.58fr; }
.responsive-study .tablet .canvas { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; grid-template-rows: 0.7fr 1fr; }
.responsive-study .phone .canvas { grid-template-columns: 1fr; grid-template-rows: 0.6fr 1fr 0.7fr; }
.responsive-study .block { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--body-color) 14%, transparent); border: 0.08em solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--body-color) 45%, transparent); }
.responsive-study .primary { background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent-color) 28%, transparent); }
.responsive-study .desktop .primary { grid-row: 1 / span 2; }
.responsive-study .tablet .primary { grid-column: 1 / span 2; }
.responsive-study .label { margin-top: 0.45em; font-size: 0.72em; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; }
</style>
<div class="responsive-study" role="img" aria-label="The same content relationships resolving across desktop, tablet and phone">
<div><div class="device desktop"><div class="bar"></div><div class="canvas"><div class="block primary"></div><div class="block"></div><div class="block"></div></div></div><div class="label">Wide viewport</div></div>
<div><div class="device tablet"><div class="bar"></div><div class="canvas"><div class="block primary"></div><div class="block"></div><div class="block"></div></div></div><div class="label">Medium viewport</div></div>
<div><div class="device phone"><div class="bar"></div><div class="canvas"><div class="block primary"></div><div class="block"></div><div class="block"></div></div></div><div class="label">Narrow viewport</div></div>
</div>
</custom-code><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="M4bBwV7RgO" colspan="30"><p>The artifact becomes a set of relationships that resolve differently on every device.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="kIhSywG36C" colspan="30"><p><b>Fixity is optional:</b> what you approve is a behaviour, not an image.</p></grid-item></grid><p font-size="11pt"><span text-color="#5D6466">Sources: <a href="https://alistapart.com/article/responsive-web-design/">A List Apart</a> · <a href="https://ethanmarcotte.com/wrote/responsive-design-at-10/">Ethan Marcotte, “Responsive Design at 10”</a></span></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="04zRlMC1W8"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="pGNse3EMQJ" vertical-align="center" colspan="22"><p role="eyebrow">OpenType 1.8 · 14 September 2016</p><h2>2016: type stops being a set of objects and becomes a space</h2><display size="lg">64,000</display><h4>Axes of variation permitted</h4><p>Up to this many axes can exist. Named instances such as Bold are coordinates the designer chose to label.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="JC8HN96wTQ" vertical-align="center" colspan="38"><h3>A single font file behaves like multiple fonts</h3><custom-code>
        <style>
          .axis-space { height: 12.5em; font-family: var(--body-font); color: var(--body-color); padding: 1.1em; border: 0.08em solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--body-color) 35%, transparent); }
          .axis-space .axis { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 6em 1fr; align-items: center; gap: 1em; margin: 1.25em 0; }
          .axis-space .rail { position: relative; height: 0.55em; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--body-color) 15%, transparent); }
          .axis-space .range { position: absolute; inset: 0 18% 0 10%; background: var(--accent-color); }
          .axis-space .point { position: absolute; width: 1.25em; height: 1.25em; border-radius: 50%; background: var(--heading-color); top: 50%; transform: translate(-50%,-50%); }
          .axis-space .a .point { left: 28%; }
          .axis-space .b .point { left: 64%; }
          .axis-space .c .point { left: 44%; }
          .axis-space .note { font-size: 0.78em; opacity: 0.75; margin-top: 1.5em; }
        </style>
        <div class="axis-space" role="img" aria-label="Three authored variation axes with selected coordinates">
          <div class="axis a"><b>Axis A</b><div class="rail"><div class="range"></div><div class="point"></div></div></div>
          <div class="axis b"><b>Axis B</b><div class="rail"><div class="range"></div><div class="point"></div></div></div>
          <div class="axis c"><b>Axis C</b><div class="rail"><div class="range"></div><div class="point"></div></div></div>
          <div class="note">The type designer authors the interpolation. The context selects a coordinate.</div>
        </div>
      </custom-code><p>This is Gerstner's programme shipped as a file format: you deliver the space, and the context picks the instance.</p><p role="caption">Announced by Apple, Google, Microsoft and Adobe at ATypI Warsaw. Sources: <a href="https://opensource.googleblog.com/2016/09/introducing-opentype-font-variations.html">Google</a> · <a href="https://blog.typekit.com/2016/09/14/variable-fonts-a-new-kind-of-font-for-flexible-design/">Adobe Typekit</a> · <a href="https://blog.fontlab.com/2016/09/14/fontlab-opentype-variations/">FontLab</a></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="KgN26aVZMg" background-color="palette-4"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="jg8bciHna1" vertical-align="center" colspan="36"><p role="eyebrow">Media regime three</p><h1>III. Computational intelligence: the model becomes the medium</h1><p font-size="lg">The rule can no longer be read directly. The designer specifies intent and judges output produced through a statistical prior.</p><p role="caption">Conceptual study</p></grid-item><grid-item id="RwlsqE7-SC" padding="0" colspan="24"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/generated-images/XPW-OJMIoX_lKzhkMki5k.png" saved-media-id="qi83d4n5b2909ji" source="image.ai-generated" fill="true" image-style="editorial-media-study" alt="Large-format editorial studio photograph of a statistical model as a material mechanism: one dark central mass surrounded by many neutral modular frames and possible image planes, with a designer's hand selecting one vermilion-edged output, cool raking daylight, carbon black, archive buff and screen grey on warm paper, conceptual rather than technical, no text or lettering" meta="{&quot;width&quot;:1248,&quot;height&quot;:1664}"></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="P1iikXRIwQ"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="cexMGJi2bw"><h2>What actually changed: the specification stopped being explicit</h2><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="fill"><grid-item id="13hCVnTWoD" background-color="palette-3" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Deterministic systems</p><h3>Read the rule, predict the output</h3><p>A grid, a stylesheet and a variable font describe explicit relationships. Given the same state, they resolve according to inspectable instructions.</p><labels><label variant="outline">Drawing → ruling</label></labels></grid-item><grid-item id="7FnwXG2w8Q" background-color="accent" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Statistical systems</p><h3>Prompt the model, judge the candidates</h3><p>A generative model is fitted to prior images and text. Its rule is not inspectable, and the same input need not repeat.</p><labels><label variant="outline">Prompting → curating → rejecting</label></labels></grid-item></grid><aside><p>Production capacity becomes nearly free. <b>Judgment about which output is right becomes the bottleneck.</b> This changes the cost structure of making; it does not show that taste has been automated.</p></aside></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="sN8gNzPPAm"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="OLYD8HldQP"><h2>The new failure mode is plausibility, not error</h2><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="_mHTy576kL" cell-style="outline" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Earlier failure</p><h3>A mistake you can point to</h3><p>The wrong sort, a broken rule, a missing image, a page that does not print. The error is local and demonstrable.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="xxV0y3dDPO" background-color="palette-2" colspan="30"><p role="eyebrow">Model failure</p><h3>Work that is merely plausible</h3><p>The corpus pulls output toward its centre of gravity: competent, familiar, averaged, and difficult to reject in a meeting.</p></grid-item></grid><h3>Three open problems you will be paid to have opinions about</h3><grid spacing="medium" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="uEoNd-xHVi" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><h4>Provenance</h4><p>Whose training material made the output possible?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="KLHwpgfcAX" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><h4>Homogenization</h4><p>What happens when visual culture converges on the average?</p></grid-item><grid-item id="VFyBe3RFnc" border="lg solid" border-sides="top" colspan="20"><h4>Accountability</h4><p>Who answers for output nobody composed?</p></grid-item></grid><p><b>The counter-move is specificity:</b> brief against the average, bring material the corpus does not contain, and edit hard.</p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="oFS2a-O04N"><grid spacing="none"><grid-item id="fStkkCgVMm"><h2>Three regimes, one direction of travel</h2><table col-widths="[19,27,27,27]" header-row="true" header-col="true" borders="rows" striped="false" style="--table-cell-padding: 5pt 12pt;"><tbody><tr><td><p></p></td><td><p>European metal-type print<br>(1455–1980s)</p></td><td><p>Digital<br>(1985–2016)</p></td><td background-color="palette-2"><p>Computational<br>(2016– )</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Unit of work</p></td><td><p>The cast letter, the printed forme</p></td><td><p>The file, the stylesheet, the component</p></td><td background-color="palette-2"><p>The prompt, the dataset, the model</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>What the designer delivers</p></td><td><p>A finished artifact</p></td><td><p>A rule that generates artifacts</p></td><td background-color="palette-2"><p>An intent plus a selection</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Where control is lost</p></td><td><p>To the press and the trades</p></td><td><p>To the device and the viewport</p></td><td background-color="palette-2"><p>To the model's statistical prior</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>What becomes cheap</p></td><td><p>Copies</p></td><td><p>Production and revision</p></td><td background-color="palette-2"><p>Composition itself</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>What becomes scarce</p></td><td><p>Access to the means</p></td><td><p>Craft attention at scale</p></td><td background-color="palette-2"><p>Judgment, framing, and editing</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p><b>Read the bottom row across:</b> every revolution has relocated scarcity, never abolished it.</p><p font-size="11pt"><span text-color="muted">The regime dates are European and industrial markers for this lecture's argument, not claims about where each technique first appeared.</span></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="vC6Tb7FKOJ"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="fAgZAgg0Ms" vertical-align="center" colspan="19"><display size="md" font-size="36pt">1925–1994</display><h4>Muriel Cooper</h4><p>A designer who treated print, screen, tools and feedback as one continuous problem.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="mvyy0_Suyu" colspan="41"><h2>Muriel Cooper designed the transition</h2><grid divider="sm neutral-border" marker="number" marker-position="inline" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" direction="vertical" style="--marker-inline-size: 30pt; --marker-gap: 14pt;"><grid-item id="tvpfJatTZc" padding="10pt 0"><h4>Four decades at MIT</h4><p>First designer in MIT’s Office of Publications; then MIT Press design director.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="1h9ostkGyK" padding="10pt 0"><h4>Mid-1970s · Visible Language Workshop</h4><p>Co-founded the emerging-media workshop; later became Media Lab founding faculty.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="O5229dOOov" padding="10pt 0"><h4>1994 · <i>Information Landscapes</i></h4><p>Typography moved, scaled and responded; shown in the year she died.</p></grid-item></grid><blockquote><p>Her consistent concern was giving designers <b>greater control and quicker feedback</b>, eventually aided by machine intelligence.</p></blockquote><p font-size="11pt"><span text-color="#5D6466">Why she closes the argument: Cooper established metaphors and trained designers who shaped the digital landscape we inherit. Source: <a href="https://core.ac.uk/download/161459016.pdf">Muriel Cooper research paper, CORE</a></span></p></grid-item></grid></section>
<section image-layout="blank" id="eMr2DotPHV" vertical-align="center" background-color="accent"><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item id="lYzfqFpEUZ" vertical-align="center" colspan="20"><p role="eyebrow">Closing argument</p><h1>What remains your job</h1><display size="md">Decision remains.</display><p>Five centuries of this history have automated <b>execution</b> repeatedly and <b>decision</b> never.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="7p_vWcBPv1" vertical-align="center" colspan="40"><grid divider="sm palette-2" marker="number" marker-position="inline" marker-numbering-format="decimal-leading-zero" direction="vertical" style="--marker-inline-size: 30pt; --marker-gap: 14pt;"><grid-item id="8jooWrrUR1" padding="10pt 0"><h4>Learn the grain</h4><p>Each transition rewarded designers who learned the new medium instead of defending the previous medium's finish.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="xG0mxJBbMe" padding="10pt 0"><h4>Know the system</h4><p>Know what it generates, what it cannot, and where control has moved.</p></grid-item><grid-item id="xGRehRiEAL" padding="10pt 0"><h4>Defend the selection</h4><p>Be able to say why this output, and not the plausible one beside it.</p></grid-item></grid><grid spacing="none" vertical-sizing="hug"><grid-item id="U83qfUTD7m" background-color="surface"><p role="eyebrow">Seminar question</p><h3>The 42-line Bible imitated the manuscript, the first web pages imitated the printed page, and generated images imitate photography and illustration.</h3><p><b>What is the native form of a computational medium, and who decides?</b></p></grid-item></grid></grid-item></grid></section>